# NEWUOA

NEWUOA is a local optimizer for smooth functions whose derivatives are
unavailable. It builds a quadratic model from objective values at a set of
nearby points, then searches within a trust region. You supply the function and
a starting point; the solver maintains the interpolation points and the model.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/newuoa/struct.Newuoa.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                  |
  | ----------- | -------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A smooth scalar function                     |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction`                               |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point               |
  | Constraints | Unconstrained                                |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Consider NEWUOA when function evaluations are expensive enough to justify
maintaining a quadratic model, and an analytic gradient is unavailable.
[Powell (2006)](#ref-powell-newuoa-2006) describes the method and recommends
using `2n + 1` interpolation points for `n` parameters, reducing the initial
sampling compared with a fully determined quadratic.

Use [BOBYQA](../bobyqa/) when the variables have box bounds, or
[LINCOA](../lincoa/) for linear inequalities. NEWUOA ignores constraint
declarations. If a reliable gradient is available, compare
[BFGS](../../quasi-newton/bfgs/) or
[L-BFGS](../../quasi-newton/l-bfgs/). For nonsmooth functions, compare
[MADS](../mads/).

## How it works

The quadratic model interpolates the stored objective values. With fewer
points than a full quadratic needs, the remaining freedom is resolved by
minimizing the Frobenius norm of the change to its Hessian. A trust-region
subproblem proposes a step, and the agreement between predicted and actual
improvement controls the next trust-region radius. These are the central
ideas in [Powell (2006)](#ref-powell-newuoa-2006).

Basin also takes geometry steps to improve the arrangement of interpolation
points. Such evaluations can improve the model without improving the current
objective. One solver iteration can include both a trial step and a geometry
step, so iterations and function evaluations are different budgets.

The solver maintains a resolution `rho` separately from the trust-region
radius. It reduces this resolution in stages and finishes after completing
the stage at the configured final radius.

## Example

This quadratic has its minimum at `[1.0, -2.0]`. The example supplies only
objective values, chooses the initial and final resolutions, and places an
evaluation budget on the executor.

<!-- compile: solver-newuoa -->
```rust
use basin::{CostFunction, Executor, Newuoa, Termination};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + 4.0 * (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = Newuoa::new()
        .with_initial_radius(0.5)
        .with_final_radius(1e-8);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Quadratic, solver, vec![-3.0, 5.0])
        .max_cost_evals(500)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    assert!(result.best_cost() < 1e-12);
    assert!((result.best_param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.best_param()[1] + 2.0).abs() < 1e-6);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Choose radii in parameter units

`with_initial_radius` sets the initial resolution and trust-region radius;
its default is `1.0`. Choose a useful initial displacement for your variables,
and rescale variables with very different units. `with_final_radius` defaults
to `1e-6` and must satisfy `initial_radius > final_radius > 0`. Finishing the
final stage records a convergence condition; it does not certify a global
minimum or a particular parameter error.

`with_absolute_radius_tolerance` provides an optional early stopping test on
the current `rho`. It is disabled by default, and it does not change the
radius schedule. Keep evaluation budgets on the executor and inspect the
termination report to distinguish completion from an exhausted budget.

### Account for interpolation work

`with_npt` chooses the interpolation-set size. Basin currently implements
`2n + 1 <= npt <= (n + 1)(n + 2) / 2`, with `2n + 1` as the default. The
smaller partial-model sizes allowed by the original algorithm are not yet
implemented. Initialization evaluates all `npt` points. Larger sets therefore
require more initial evaluations and more model storage and work.

### Match the resolution to the objective

Interpolation models assume that nearby function values contain useful local
information. Noise, discontinuities, and a final radius below meaningful
numerical resolution can undermine that information. Choose radii for the
objective's accuracy and the scalar precision; the example's `1e-8` is an
`f64` choice for an exact quadratic.

## Implementation

NEWUOA publishes the current evaluated point and its historical incumbent
through `PointState`. The solver owns the quadratic model, interpolation
geometry, and radius schedule. Fresh initialization rebuilds the model; exact
solver-and-state checkpoints preserve it. Use `Executor::run_with_solver` to
retain the final solver and inspect `rho()` or `geometry_diagnostics()`. The
diagnostic gradient comes from the interpolation model, rather than an
evaluation of the objective's gradient.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The model
uses internal vectors and pure-Rust algebra, so no matrix capability or
BLAS/LAPACK provider is required from the parameter backend. Basin's model
updates and driver have been cross-validated against
[PRIMA](https://github.com/libprima/prima) v0.7.2.

